diff --git a/docs/chat-bots.md b/docs/chat-bots.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..97dd214
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/chat-bots.md
@@ -0,0 +1,74 @@
+# Chat bots
+
+To create a bot, open **Account settings**, select **Set up chat bots**, then select **Add a chat bot**. The bots page shows the codes for the bots you manage. Administrators can manage all bots.
+
+Members can create up to five bots each by default. Administrators can change **Bots per member** in **Account settings** (set it to zero to stop members from creating bots). Lowering the limit keeps existing bots, but members can't create more until they're below the limit. Deleted bots don't count, and administrators aren't limited.
+
+By default, bots do not join any rooms. To give a bot access to a specific open or closed room, select it in that room’s membership settings. Turn on **Add to all open rooms** on the bot’s form to add it to existing open rooms and any rooms that become open later. Turning the switch off keeps the bot’s current memberships.
+
+Post text to a room the bot belongs to:
+
+```sh
+curl --data-binary 'Deploy of v1.2.3 finished ✅' \
+  "https://chat.example.com/rooms/${ROOM_ID}/${BOT_CODE}/messages"
+```
+
+The request body is the message text. The API returns `201 Created` with `Location: /messages/:id`, `422` for an empty message, `404` for an unknown or inaccessible room, or `401` for an invalid or revoked code.
+
+Treat the bot code like a password. Use HTTPS and store the code in your CI or shell secret store. Campfire redacts it from Rails request logs, but reverse proxies and external access loggers may see the original URL. Configure those services to redact or skip logging these request paths. Deleting a bot revokes its code without removing its previous messages.
+
+## Cooldowns and suspensions
+
+A bot can post up to 10 messages in a rolling 10-second window across all its rooms. API posts and webhook replies share this limit. Exceeding it rejects the message and starts a 30-second cooldown. During the cooldown, API posts return `429 Too Many Requests` with a `Retry-After` header giving the number of seconds to wait. Webhooks still deliver, but their replies aren't posted. Five more attempts to post during that cooldown suspend the bot.
+
+Ten consecutive failed webhook deliveries also suspend the bot. Any `2xx` response resets the failure count, even if it doesn't produce a reply. Other response codes, timeouts, and connection errors count as failures.
+
+The bots page shows **Suspended** and the reason. A suspended bot can't receive webhooks, and its posting code returns `403 Forbidden`. It keeps its rooms and messages and still counts towards its owner's bot limit. An administrator can select **Reactivate** on the bots page and confirm to clear its moderation counters and resume posting and webhook delivery without changing the bot code.
+
+## Receive events by webhook
+
+Set **Webhook URL** on the bot's form to receive HTTP `POST` callbacks when the bot joins a room or is @-mentioned. Pick the bot from the room’s @-mention suggestions; typing the name as plain text does not notify it. Clear the field to turn callbacks off.
+
+Campfire queues delivery after the message or membership is saved, so the person using Campfire does not wait for your endpoint. The bot must be active and belong to the room.
+
+The request has `Content-Type: application/json`.
+
+### Mentions
+
+Repeated mentions of the same bot in one message generate one callback, and messages posted by bots do not trigger mention callbacks.
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "mention",
+  "author": { "id": 7, "name": "Alice" },
+  "room": { "id": 5, "name": "Ops", "reply_to": "/rooms/5/42-Xy3abc9defGh/messages" },
+  "message": {
+    "id": 118,
+    "body": {
+      "text": "restart the build please",
+      "html": "<div>… full rich-text body …</div>"
+    },
+    "link": "/rooms/5/@118"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+`message.body.text` omits mentions of the receiving bot; `message.body.html` includes the original mentions and formatting. Both `message.link` and `room.reply_to` are paths relative to your Campfire origin. **Keep `room.reply_to` private:** it contains the bot's posting credential.
+
+### Joining a room
+
+When a bot is added to an open or closed room, Campfire sends a `joined` event. It attempts delivery only once per bot and room, even if the bot later leaves and rejoins. Direct conversations do not send `joined` events, and setting a webhook URL does not send events for existing memberships.
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "joined",
+  "room": { "id": 5, "name": "Ops", "reply_to": "/rooms/5/42-Xy3abc9defGh/messages" }
+}
+```
+
+### Replying to either event
+
+- To reply immediately, return `200 OK` with `Content-Type: text/plain` or `text/html` and a UTF-8 reply body (up to 1 MiB). Campfire sanitizes HTML before posting it.
+- To acknowledge without posting, return `204 No Content`. To reply later, send the message text in a `POST` to the supplied `room.reply_to` path as shown above.
+
+Use a public HTTPS endpoint for deployed bots. Campfire does not send callbacks to private network addresses or follow redirects. In the Rails development environment only, you can use a loopback URL to test a bot locally. If the endpoint fails, times out, or returns an unsupported reply, the original message or membership is unaffected and no reply is posted.
diff --git a/lib/middleware/bot_message_request.rb b/lib/middleware/bot_message_request.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..0103f87
--- /dev/null
+++ b/lib/middleware/bot_message_request.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,26 @@
+class BotMessageRequest
+  PATH = %r{\A(/+rooms/+[^/]+/+)([^/]+)(/+messages/*)\z}
+
+  def initialize(app)
+    @app = app
+  end
+
+  def call(env)
+    if match = PATH.match(env["PATH_INFO"])
+      # Move the credential out of the URL before the request reaches Rails logging or telemetry.
+      env["campfire.bot_code"] = Rack::Utils.unescape_path(match[2])
+      filtered_path = "#{match[1]}[FILTERED]#{match[3]}"
+      %w[ PATH_INFO REQUEST_PATH REQUEST_URI ORIGINAL_FULLPATH ORIGINAL_PATH_INFO ].each do |key|
+        env[key] = env[key].sub(match[0]) { filtered_path } if env[key]
+      end
+
+      # The body is text, including when curl -d labels it as form data.
+      if env["REQUEST_METHOD"] == "POST"
+        env["CONTENT_TYPE"] = "text/plain"
+        env.delete("HTTP_X_HTTP_METHOD_OVERRIDE")
+      end
+    end
+
+    @app.call(env)
+  end
+end
